- Occult osteomyelitis (if bone is exposed or probe-to-bone positive);  
- Squamous cell carcinoma (especially if wound has atypical features like induration, nodularity, or rapid change — though patient denies these);  
- Fungal or atypical infection (less common, but possible in chronic wounds).  
Given the absence of systemic symptoms and stable course, malignancy is lower probability — but biopsy remains the gold standard to exclude it. Since wound appearance details are missing, tissue diagnosis is the most definitive next step to confirm etiology and guide therapy.

